  <h1>Wu Chi <span class="tag tag-beginner">Beginner · Position 1</span></h1>

  <p>Wu Chi is the starting point and foundation of all Zhan Zhuang. "Wu Chi" means the state of formlessness and undifferentiated chaos — standing here, like a tree, completely still on the outside yet growing slowly and powerfully within. This is the first posture in Master Lam's system, and the root of all subsequent postures.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📖 Book Reference</h4>
    <p>《The Way of Energy》Part One, Chapters 1-3: Learning to Stand / Breathing and Relaxing / Internal Movement</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📺 BBC Reference</h4>
    <p>《Stand Still Be Fit》Days 1-2: Wu Chi instruction and deepening</p>
  </div>

  ---

  <h2>Practice Guide: Step by Step</h2>

  <h3>Step 1: Feet</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Feet parallel, shoulder-width apart</li>
    <li>Toes pointing forward, gripping the ground like tree roots</li>
    <li>Weight evenly distributed across both feet, neither forward nor back</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 2: Knees</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Naturally slightly bent, not locked straight</li>
    <li><strong>Knees must not extend beyond the toes</strong>, otherwise knee injury may result</li>
    <li>Bend only to a comfortable degree — do not force yourself to squat lower</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 3: Torso</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Spine naturally upright — do not puff out the chest or stick out the buttocks</li>
    <li>Imagine sitting on an invisible balloon chair — <strong>sitting-not-sitting</strong></li>
    <li>Tailbone centered, pelvis in a neutral position</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 4: Arms and Armpits</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Hands naturally hanging at your sides</li>
    <li>Palms facing inward, fingers slightly spread</li>
    <li>Key point: imagine holding a small balloon under each armpit, slightly propping the arms up — <strong>avoid pressing them flat against the body</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 5: Head</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Head suspended — imagine the crown floating lightly above the shoulders</li>
    <li>Gaze forward, eyes calm and peaceful</li>
    <li>Chin slightly tucked, neck relaxed</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 6: Breathing</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Breathe naturally and quietly through the nose</li>
    <li>Breathing should be deep, long, natural, and silent</li>
    <li>Do not deliberately control the breath — let it be natural</li>
  </ul>

  ---

  <h2>Mental Imagery</h2>
  <p>Mental imagery is a core feature of Master Lam's system. The following images are used in Wu Chi:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Image</th><th>Function</th><th>Key Point</th></tr>
    <tr><td>🎈 Balloon chair</td><td>Guides the pelvis and center of gravity</td><td>Imagine a balloon under the pelvis supporting the body — sitting-not-sitting, with a stable center</td></tr>
    <tr><td>🎈 Armpit balloons</td><td>Props up the arms</td><td>Imagine holding a small balloon under each armpit — arms neither pressed flat nor raised</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Head suspended</td><td>Guides the neck and spine</td><td>Imagine the crown floating above the shoulders, the neck naturally lengthening</td></tr>
  </table>

